 # Deep Learning and Neural Architectures
 
+Also called: DL-based Tomographic Reconstruction, Neural Inverse Solvers
 
 ## Overview
-This family encompasses methods that utilize neural networks to solve the inverse problem of tomographic reconstruction, mapping measured sinograms to 3D volumes. It distinguishes itself from classical iterative methods by replacing explicit regularization or fixed filters with learned representations or differentiable end-to-end mappings. Unlike [Deep Image Prior Reconstruction](1221-deep-image-prior-reconstruction.md) which optimizes a network per instance, or [R2-Gaussian](6090-r2-gaussian.md) which uses explicit geometric primitives, this family includes generalizable end-to-end models [7504-end-to-end-reconstruction-model-architecture.md], hierarchical domain transforms [10853-hierarchical-ct-reconstruction-via-domain-transform.md], and attention-based architectures [13148-transformer-based-architectures.md].
+This family encompasses methods that utilize deep neural networks to solve the inverse problem of tomographic reconstruction, mapping measured projection data (sinograms) directly or iteratively to a 3D volume estimate. Unlike classical analytical methods (e.g., Filtered Back-Projection) or iterative algebraic techniques, these methods leverage the expressive power of neural architectures to regularize the ill-posed nature of the inverse Radon transform, particularly in sparse-view or noisy regimes. They are distinguished from [Neural Radiance Fields and Implicit Neural Representations](13341-neural-radiance-fields-and-implicit-neural-representations.md) by their explicit focus on tomographic geometry (Radon transforms) and often their ability to produce explicit voxel-based or parametric density volumes suitable for quantitative analysis, rather than just view-dependent rendering.
 
